From e4384f70739adbacef3638133560c704f83ab29d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nasser Jabir <nasser.jabir@nemons.local>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 20:33:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 09/11] feat(storage,daemon): the memory sharing scope column,
 server-side privacy

The learnings table gains scope (shared|private|public, YM-378), the central
read judges it at the WHERE level (LearningsFilter.Viewer; system reads pass
'' byte-identically), GetLearningForViewer hides behind the literal no-rows
shape, the briefing reads through its agent's eyes, remember judges the RAW
scope value before any normalization (private needs its caller, public needs
the memory.public_write standing grant, unknown refused fail-closed, cap rows
stay shared), and a supersede never lands on a row the caller cannot see
while a truly-missing target keeps upstream's behaviour. The governed
registry starts at the governance ops themselves: upstream memory ops keep
their own behaviour.
